<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<handout>
  <title>Section 3.4: Logarithms</title>
  <p>
    <alert>MTH 124</alert>
  </p>

  <definition workspace="1in">
    <title>Definition 3.4.3</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Given a positive real number <m>y</m>,
 the <term>base-10 logarithm of <m>y</m></term>
 is the power to which we raise 10
 to get <m>y</m>.
 We use the notation "<m>\log_{10}(y)</m>” to denote the base-10
 logarithm of <m>y</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>Fill in the following:</p>
  <p><md>\begin{array}{ccc c ccc}
10^1 \amp  = \amp   10\amp  \Longleftrightarrow \amp \log_{10}(10) \amp  = \amp  1\\
10^2 \amp =\amp  <fillin/>\amp  \Longleftrightarrow \amp  \log_{10}(10^2) \amp  = \amp  <fillin/>\\
10^3 \amp =\amp <fillin/> \amp  \Longleftrightarrow \amp  \log_{10}(10^3) \amp  = \amp  <fillin/>\\
10^6 \amp =\amp <fillin/>\amp  \Longleftrightarrow \amp  \log_{10}(10^6) \amp  = \amp  <fillin/>\\
10^{-3} \amp =\amp <fillin/>\amp  \Longleftrightarrow \amp  \log_{10}(\frac{1}{1000}) \amp  = \amp  <fillin/>\\
10^{1.3} \amp =\amp  <fillin/>\amp  \Longleftrightarrow \amp  \log_{10}(10^{1.3}) \amp  = \amp <fillin/>\\
10^{0} \amp =\amp  <fillin/>\amp  \Longleftrightarrow \amp  \log_{10}(1) \amp  = \amp  <fillin/>\\
\end{array}</md></p>

  <assemblage>
  <p>
    <ul cols="2">
      <li>
       
        <p>
          <em>Equation:  </em> <m>y=P(t)=10^t</m>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>

        <p>
        <em>Equation:  </em> <m>t = L(y)=\log_{10}(y)</m>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Domain:
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Domain:
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Range:
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Range:
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Composition:
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Composition:
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          0 and 1:
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          0 and 1:
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>** Note 1. ** Most calculators assume base-10, so <c>log(y)</c> in a calculator is the equivalent of <m>\log_{10}(y)</m>.</p>
  <p>** Note 2. ** Some values of <m>\log_{10}(y)</m> are easier to find than others.  The value <m>\log_{10}(5)</m> is an exact number (like <m>\pi</m> or <m>\cos(1)</m>), where <m>\log_{10}(5)\approx 0.699</m>.</p>
  <p workspace="1.8in">Examples:</p>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 3.4.2</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Find the <term>exact</term> value and approximate value of the unknown variable (<m>t</m> or <m>y</m>). For instance, if the exact value is <m>y=\log_{10}(2)</m>,
 you can also note that <m>y\approx 0.301</m>.
    <ol cols="2" marker="a.">
      <li>
        <p workspace="0.6in">
          <m>10^t = 0.00001</m>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p workspace="0.6in"><m>\log_{10}(1000000) = t</m></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p workspace="0.6in"><m>10^t = 37</m></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p workspace="0.6in"><m>2\log_{10}(y)+5 = 1</m></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p workspace="0.6in"><m>\log_{10}(y) = 1.375</m></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p workspace="0.6in"><m>10^t = 0.04</m></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p workspace="0.6in"><m>3 \cdot 10^t + 11 = 147</m></p>
      </li>

      </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S3-4-soln">
      <p>
        <ol cols="2" marker="a.">
          <li>
            <p>
              This is the same as <m>10^t = 10^{-5}</m>.  So <m>t=-5</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              This is the same as <m>\log_{10}(10^6) = t</m>, which <m>t=6</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use the relationship that <m>10^a = b</m> is equivalent to <m>\log_{10}(b) = a</m>.  
              So this becomes <m>t = \log_{10}(37)</m> exactly.  Which is approximately
              <m>t\approx 1.568</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Simplify first, <m>2\log_{10}(y) = -4</m>, and then
              <m>\log_{10}(y) = -2</m>, which can be rewritten as 
              <m>y = 10^{-2} = 0.01</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Rearrange: <m>y = 10^{1.375} \approx 23.714</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Rearrange: <m>t = \log_{10}(0.04) \approx -1.398</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Solve first, <m>3 \cdot 10^t = 136</m>, then <m>10^t = \frac{136}{3}</m>, 
              which rearranges to <m>t = \log_{10}(\frac{136}{3}) \approx 1.656</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </solution>
 
  </activity>
<assemblage xml:id="assemblage-e">
  <title></title>
   <p>** Now we will consider <m>y = e^t</m> and it's inverse function <m>t = \ln(y)</m>.</p>

</assemblage>
 

  <definition>
    <title>Definition 3.4.4</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Given a positive real number <m>y</m>,
 the <term>natural logarithm of <m>y</m></term>
 is the power to which we raise <m>e</m>
 to get <m>y</m>.
 We denote "<m>\ln(y)</m>” as the natural logarithm of <m>y</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
<note>
  <p>The natural log of y is <m>\ln(y)</m>, which is shorthand for <m>\log_{e}(y)</m>.</p>

</note>
  <example>
  <p> Examples: 
    <md>\ln(e^1) = 1, \quad \quad \ln(e^{1.3}) = 1.3, \quad \quad \ln(e^{84}) = 84</md>
  </p>
 
</example>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 3.4.3</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>Let <m>E(t) = e^t</m>
 and <m>N(x)=\ln(x)</m>
 be the natural exponential function and the natural logarithm function, respectively.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.2in">
      <statement>
        <p>What are the domain and range of <m>E(t) = e^t</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S3-4-soln">
      <p>
        Domain: all real numbers or <m>(-\infty, \infty)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Range: all positive real numbers or <m>(0, \infty)</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.2in">
      <statement>
        <p>What are the domain and range of <m>N(x) = \ln(x)</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S3-4-soln">
      <p>
        Domain: all positive real numbers i.e. <m>(0,\infty)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Range: all real numbers i.e. <m>(-\infty, \infty)</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.2in">
      <statement>
        <p>What can you say about <m>\ln(e^t)</m>?  Think about inverse functions. (Note, this is the composition <m>N(E(t))</m>.)</p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S3-4-soln">
      <p>
        <m>\ln(e^t) = t</m> because inverses undo each other!
      </p>
    </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.2in">
      <statement>
        <p>What can you say about <m>e^{\ln(x)}</m>?  (Note, this is the composition <m>E(N(x))</m>.)</p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S3-4-soln">
      <p>
        <m>e^{\ln(x)} = x</m>, yay!
      </p>
    </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>Complete the tables below with both exact and approximate values (the first is done for you). 
        Then sketch both <m>y=e^x</m> and <m>y=\ln(x)</m> on the graph below.</p>
        
        <sidebyside >
          <stack>
          <p>*Values of <m>E(t) = e^t:</m></p>
            <tabular>
              <row bottom="major">
                <cell><m>t</m></cell>
                <cell><m>e^t</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
                <cell><m>e^{-2} \approx 0.135</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
                <cell></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                <cell></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                <cell></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                <cell></cell>
              </row>
            </tabular>
          </stack>

           <stack>
          <p>*Values of <m>N(y) = \ln(y)</m>:</p>
            <tabular>
              <row bottom="major">
                <cell><m>y</m></cell>
                <cell><m>\ln(y)</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>e^{-2}</m></cell>
                <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>e^{-1}</m></cell>
                <cell></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                <cell></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>e</m></cell>
                <cell></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>e^2</m></cell>
                <cell></cell>
              </row>
            </tabular>
          </stack>
          <image>
      <shortdescription>
        A blank graph to draw e^x and ln(x).
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-3-4-3">
        <diagram dimensions="(350,350)" margins="7">
          <definition>f(t)=e^t</definition>
          
          <coordinates bbox="(-8,-8,8,8)">
            <grid-axes decorations="yes"  xlabel="x" ylabel="y" spacings="((-8,1,8),(-8,1,8))"
            hlabels="(-7,1,7)" vlabels="(-7,1,7)"/>
  
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>

        </sidebyside>
        
      
        <p>(Both plots have the first row of the table as input, the second row as output.)</p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S3-4-soln">
       <sidebyside >
          <stack>
          <p>*Values of <m>E(t) = e^t:</m></p>
            <tabular>
              <row bottom="major">
                <cell><m>t</m></cell>
                <cell><m>e^t</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
                <cell><m>e^{-2} \approx 0.135</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
                <cell><m>e^{-1}\approx 0.368</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                <cell><m>e^0 = 1</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                <cell><m>e^1\approx 2.718...</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                <cell><m>e^2\approx 7.389</m></cell>
              </row>
            </tabular>
          </stack>

           <stack>
          <p>*Values of <m>N(y) = \ln(y)</m>:</p>
            <tabular>
              <row bottom="major">
                <cell><m>y</m></cell>
                <cell><m>\ln(y)</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>e^{-2}</m></cell>
                <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>e^{-1}</m></cell>
                <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                <cell><m>0</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><m>e</m></cell>
                <cell><m>1</m></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
