      </prefigure>
    </image>

        </sidebyside>
       
    </solution>
    </task>
  </activity>

<assemblage xml:id="assemblage-">
  <title>Rearranging</title>
 <p>
    The expression <m>e^a = b</m> is the same thing as the expression <m>\ln(b) = a</m>
  </p>
</assemblage>
  

  <example>
     <p>
    If we want to solve <m>e^{5t} = 7</m>, this is equivalent to <m>\ln(7) = 5t</m>. From here we can divide
    each side by 5 and get <m>t = \frac{\ln(7)}{5}</m>, which is an exact solution. 
  </p>
  <p>
    If we want to solve <m>\ln(t) = 4</m>, this is equivalent to <m>e^4 = t</m>.  Thus we have the exact solution.
  </p>
  </example>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 3.4.4</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Solve each of the following equations for the exact value of the unknown variable (<m>t</m>). If there is no solution to the equation, explain why not.</p>
        <p>
          <ol cols="2" marker="a.">
            <li>
              <p workspace="0.8in"><m>e^t = \frac{1}{10}</m></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p workspace="0.8in"><m>5e^t = 7</m></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p workspace="0.8in"><m>\ln(t)=\frac{-1}{3}</m></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p workspace="0.8in"><m>2\ln(t)+1 = 4</m></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p workspace="0.8in"><m>e^{1-3t}=4</m></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p workspace="0.8in"><m>4-3e^{2t} = 2</m></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p workspace="0.8in"><m>4+3e^{2t}=2</m></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p workspace="0.8in"><m>\ln(5-6t) = -2</m></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S3-4-soln">
      <p>
        <ol cols="2" marker="a.">
          <li>
            <p>
              Use the relationship that <m>e^a = b</m> is equivalent to 
              <m>\ln(b) = a</m>.  Here it's ready to go, rearranging gives 
              <m>t = \ln(\frac1{10})</m> which is the exact solution.  This is
              approximately <m>t\approx -2.303</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Simplifies to <m>e^t = \frac75</m>, which then rearranges to 
              <m>t = \ln(\frac75) \approx 0.336</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Rearrange, <m>t = e^{-1/3}\approx 0.717</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Simplify to <m>2\ln(t) = 3</m>, and then <m>\ln(t) = \frac32</m>, 
              and rearranges to <m>t = e^{3/2}\approx 4.482</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Rearrange right away to <m>1-3t = \ln(4)</m>, and then algebra simplifies to
              <m>-3t = \ln(4) - 1</m>, then <m>t = \frac{\ln(4) - 1}{-3}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Simplifies to <m>-3e^{2t} = -2</m>, then <m>e^{2t} = \frac23</m>, 
              then rearranges to <m>2t = \ln(\frac23)</m>. Last step: 
              <m>t = \frac12 \ln(\frac23) \approx -0.203</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Simplify to <m>3e^{2t} = -2</m>, then <m>e^{2t} = -\frac23</m>. 
              There's a problem here.  If you don't see it you could keep going, and 
              get <m>2t = \ln(-\frac23)</m>.  Here's the same problem.  You cannot 
              take the natural log of a negative number! It doesn't work!  Similarly, 
              <m>e^{anything}</m> will never be a negative number!  So this problem 
              has no solution.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Rearrange right away:
              <m>e^{-2} = 5-6t</m>.  Now solve for <m>t</m> using algebra:
              <m>{e^{-2} - 5 = -6t}</m>, then <m>{t = \frac{e^{-2}-5}{-6}}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </solution>

</activity>
<activity>
 
<introduction>
        
        <p>Example: Certain radioactive material decays in such a way that the mass remaining after 
 years is given by the function <m>m(t)</m> measured in grams, given below for some unknown values of <m>A, k</m>: <md>m(t) = Ae^{kt}</md></p>
      </introduction>
  
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>If the mass at time <m>t=0</m> is 200 grams, find <m>A</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S3-4-soln">
      <p>
        Plug in <m>t=0</m> and set <m>m(0)=200</m>, so <md>m(0) = 200 = Ae^{k*0} = A(1) = A</md>
        so <m>A = 200</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>After 15 years, 100 grams were left.  Now that we know <m>A</m> from part (a), can we use this to find <m>k</m>?  Keep your answer exact, that is, don't round!  (Leave <m>\ln(b)</m> as <m>\ln(b)</m>.)</p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S3-4-soln">
      <p>
        So <m>m(15) = 100</m>, and also then 
        <md>
          m(15) = 100 = 200 e^{k*15}.
        </md>
        We can solve that resulting equation just like in the previous activity!  Now 
        we just solve for <m>k</m> instead of <m>t</m>.  Which means we 
        can first rewrite this as 
        <m>{\frac{1}2 = e^{k*15}}</m>.  Now we can rearrange to 
        <md>
          \ln(\frac12) = k*15.
        </md>
        Now finally, <m>{k = \frac{1}{15}\ln(\frac12)}</m>.  That is 
        the exact solution, but this 
        is approximately <m>k\approx -0.04621</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now you know <m>A</m> and <m>k</m>, you know the whole model. How much mass is left after 20 years?
        </p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S3-4-soln">
      <p>
        We are looking for <m>m(20)</m>, and our model is 
        <m>{m(t) = 200e^{\frac1{15}\ln(\frac12) * t}}</m>, so we just plug <m>t</m> in.
        <md>
          m(20) = 200e^{\frac1{15}\ln(\frac12) * 20}.
        </md>
        This can simplify technically, but this is a number.  It turns out to 
        be approximately <m>79.37</m> grams.
      </p>
    </solution>
    </task>
  </activity>

</handout>
